# Token discipline (Go)

## Read (input)

- Only read paths the user named (or clearly implied). No workspace-wide Grep/Glob/explore tours.
- Review exception (`code-review` skill only): Grep allowed for symbols the diff adds, renames, or whose error/return type changed. Same Go module only.
- Blocked without a path? Stop and ask. Do not guess-search the tree.
- Other Go services / packages: read only the handler, DTO, or route needed for the API contract. Do not traverse repository layers or migration history unless the task requires it.
- Prefer `AGENTS.md`, Cursor rules, and live code (`routes.go`, handlers, RPC) over rediscovering contracts by touring the tree.
- No Task/subagents for narrow implement-from-plan or single-file work.

## Write / reply (output)

- No filler, greetings, or “let me know if…”. Max 2–3 sentences of reasoning when needed.
- Edits via tools; in chat, show only changed hunks (`// ... existing code ...`), never whole files.
- No drive-by refactors, speculative docs, or “also polish.”
- No tests or test runs unless the user explicitly asks. When verification is in scope, follow `claims-and-verification.mdc`.
- No new boilerplate configs (Docker/Makefile/CI scaffolding) unless asked.
- No PR unless the user asks; if asked, smoke-compile first (`go build ./...` or package scope), then offload full lint/tests to CI and fix from workflow logs.
- Final reply: short file list + how to verify.
